CPP Module 05

STD::EXCEPTION

Std::exception is a class holding all sorts of exceptions. When we throw an exception-object is created and stores the exception there. The stack starts to unwind fully, unless there’s a try catch block. If there is, it will go to the closest try catch block and you can handle it however you want (continue the program, exit program neatly). Lets say we ask the user for input but it’s wrong, we can use a try catch block to catch wrong input, and prompt the user to try again. Or we have a try catch block in the very beginning trying to run the program, if something throws we catch it at the very beginning of the program simply to output the error message and exit properly.

The exception class has a lot of exceptions, so if we use the latter way of trying to catch at the beginning of the program, we are prepared to catch any throw. Whereas if we create our own class to handle specific exception cases, we would miss out on a lot of the exceptions from the exception class. Fine if we know what we can throw and we want to catch that specific thing. Not very useful for trying to catch a broad lot of exceptions.


INHERITANCE

Let’s say we have a student class. We could have a Bob/Mia/Fiona/etc. Class but they all inherit from the student class with its public functions. We might define a function in both of these classes. Void announce(); So there would be two functions in Bob with the same name. So which do we call upon?

If we call on Bob we will of course first look into Bob's functions and call the announce function defined in Bob. If we have a Bob pointer we do the same. Now if we have a bunch of student pointers (holding Bob, Mia, Fiona, etc.) the compiler only sees them as student pointers. However, we still want each object to call its own custom announce() function. That's when we make the function virtual. A virtual function tells C++ that before calling the function, it should determine at runtime what type of object it is actually pointing to, and if a derived class has overridden that function, call that version instead.


