STATIC

To make a class static we cannot actually do it but we can make it behave like such. When having a static variable or function it belongs to the class itself, not an instance of the class. Static functions can thus only operate on static variables as they don’t belong to an instance and don’t know which instance to operate on. We don’t have that problem with static variables as they also don’t belong to a class, the classes just have access to that same variable.

To make a static class we want it to not be instantiable, thus making it that we cannot call its constructor, by either making them private, or using the delete keyword.


When creating an object from a class we always have this implicit *this pointer. When working inside class function and accessing class variables, the *this pointer implicitly gets added to the front to specify which variable form which instance we’re talking about.

With nested types such as structs it works a little differently. Nested types are not stored in the class object itself but live in its scope. Meaning they are class scope and not object scoped. They hold no data (so we will never refer to it with the *this pointer) but only give us information about a certain structure. Accessing it we must do so through the class name. Not an implicit *this pointer.


BUILDING AN EXECUTABLE(USING PRAGMA ONCE)

It starts with the Preprocessor. This takes care of text manipulation. So purely finding/replacing text and copying/pasting text. It puts everything (including the includes) into a bigger cpp file(The Translation Unit). So one unit per cpp file.
The Compiler Proper takes these big texts and parses it as C++, meaning: checking syntax, types, functions, etc. And turns it into assembly/object code.
(So it will create a mental note of definitions like lets say classes. Once we get to the main where we actually create something (lets say a variable) we look into our notes and create this variable. If we encounter templates we get the function definition from our mental notes and copy this with the correct T type. And eventually we can turn this into object code.)
The Assembler takes the assembly code and turns this into machine code, producing object files (.o files).
The Linker links all the seperate .o files to each other and turns this into the final executable. Now u can run.


When pragma once is encountered it will store the file name and keep track of this. When not yet encountered it will paste the contents, but if so it will skip this step making sure theres no double inclusion within a single translation unit. Ofcourse with more cpp files header can be included more often.


STD::TIME STD::RAND

To get a random number we can use the rand() function. You can pass it a random number and it will do some randomizing calculations on top of this number, resulting in a new random number. It will continue doing this with the new number it generated. If no starting number is given there will be a set number. So in the end it’s not so random.

To avoid always having the same number as starting point (whether you hardcoded it or its the standard set number) you can use the static_cast<unsigned int>(std::time(NULL) to generate the current time to an unsigned int. Use this as a starting number and it is a lot more random.

Ex:

Bool seeded = false;

If (!seeded)

	srand() = static_cast<unsigned int>(std::time(NULL)

