CASTING

Instead of c’s way of casting: (float)3, in c++ there are three main ways of casting.

STATIC CAST

Creates a (temporary) new variable by conversion. It’s cast during compile time so checked by the compiler but never during runtime.

REINTERPRET CAST

Doesn’t change any of the bits but tells the compiler to look at it in a different way. So let’s say instead of looking at the bits like an int, like look at the first bit and interpret it as a char. Also is not checked during runtime so both this and static cast it dangerous if not used properly.

DYNAMIC CAST

Dynamic cast works specifically for polymorphic classes. They require the base class to have at least one virtual function. Upon having this the function gives the object a vptr(virtual pointer) that points to a vtable(virtual table). During runtime when needing to access the virtual function it will point us to this vtable where we can access the RTTI(runtime type information) and through this we have access to whatever the derived type is. Because it can check during runtime it’s safe. You can make a guess and dynamically cast it, if it’s wrong it will return a null pointer.


EXCEPTION ORDER

Why do we need to define an exception as a const std::exception& e? And how does it know the error?

As soon as something throws it creates an exception object. This is the current exception object being handled. The runtime keeps track of the active exception. Now the program will go to the closest catch block to see how it’s handled. We catch the reference to the exception block because it's more resourceful to utilise a reference than it is to make a copy of it.

Now lets say we are in a nested exception loop where multiple exception objects can exist. The runtime still knows which exception block is currently being handled and it will bind the correct reference block to it.



TEMPLATES

Templates are a feature in c++ where a function can receive lots of different types of variables, as long as it compiles correctly (the given type must be used with the right syntax in the function).

template<typename T, typename F) void func(T a, T* b, F c) {}

We specify the different types of variables we wanna take in which could be anything. We can put T as a function, as a pointer, just as an int. As long as it’s used appropriately.

template<typename T, typename F>
void iter(T* ptr, const int length, F func)
{
	for (int i = 0; i < length; i++)
	{
		func(ptr[i]);
	}
}

In ex02 from mod7 above we see we call the function with whatever is located at ptr[i]. The compiler checks this at compile time. If two types dont allign (lets say the function wants string but is passes int) its gonna give us the error.